## How it works

TSE (Timed Spec Engine) runs serial protocols that are written as timed specifications. A timed spec describes one side of a protocol: its states, what it drives on which wire and the timing windows from the datasheet. A compiler on a PC turns the spec into programs and configuration words for this chip, works out when every output edge and every sample happens, and checks each one against its window. If a window cannot be met, the compiler rejects the spec with the reason and a suggested fix, and produces no image.

The chip runs an image in three ways:

- Speak: it acts as a controller or a target of a protocol on its pins.
- Check: it watches a bus between other devices, timestamps what happens and reports broken timing rules. The host can move one threshold a clock at a time to measure a device's margin.
- Trigger: it decodes the traffic and pulses an output pin when a chosen sequence of events happens.

Inside the tile:

- Two lanes. A lane is a small processor with 16-bit instructions and exact cycle timing, plus hardware assists: a CRC of 5 to 32 bits, a bit stuffer, NRZI and Manchester codecs, a clock-recovery DPLL with samples on both clock edges, timers and two response rules that answer inside a bit. The two lanes can run two protocols, or both sides of one, at the same time.
- One SRAM macro of 512 words of 32 bits holds both programs and a data window. Every lane fetches in its own fixed time slots, so one lane cannot change the other's timing.
- A pin crossbar connects the four virtual pins of each lane to any pad, with open drain on the `uio` pads and an idle level for every pad, so a UART line is high and an I2C bus is released before any lane runs. A lane can also read pads the other lane drives.
- An event unit timestamps events from both lanes on one counter, keeps them in a FIFO for the host, and pulses the trigger pin when a sequence of up to three stages matches.
- A loader writes the SRAM and the configuration, runs an SRAM self-test (March C-) and checks a CRC-32 over the whole image. If the SRAM self-test fails, the host can load a short program into a small fallback store instead.

The project clock is 60 MHz. The design also runs at 50 MHz: the compiler takes the clock as an input, every image records the clock it was compiled for, and the host library sets the project clock from the image.

### Two states: load mode and run mode

The `rst_n` pin selects the state.

- Load mode (`rst_n` low): the lanes, crossbar outputs and event unit are held in reset and every `uio` pin is an input. The host writes bytes on `ui_in[7:0]`; every change of `uio[0]` (the strobe) delivers one byte, and `uio[1]` high marks the first byte of a command. The chip shows its readback byte on `uo_out[7:0]`.
- Run mode (`rst_n` high): the pins belong to the crossbar, except the runtime SPI interface and, if enabled, the interrupt and trigger pins. The host starts lanes by writing the `RUN` register over SPI.

A tile that is deselected on the shuttle loses its power and comes back with random contents, so the host loads the chip after every selection. The host keeps `rst_n` low after selection; entering load mode clears the loaded flag, and `RUN` is refused until a load has passed its CRC check, so random contents never run.

### Pins

| Pin | Load mode (`rst_n` low) | Run mode (`rst_n` high) |
|---|---|---|
| `ui_in[0]` | loader data bit 0 | host SPI CS_N, or crossbar input |
| `ui_in[1]` | loader data bit 1 | host SPI SCK, or crossbar input |
| `ui_in[2]` | loader data bit 2 | host SPI MOSI, or crossbar input |
| `ui_in[7:3]` | loader data bits 3 to 7 | crossbar inputs |
| `uo_out[2:0]` | readback bits 0 to 2 | crossbar outputs |
| `uo_out[3]` | readback bit 3 | host SPI MISO, or crossbar output |
| `uo_out[5:4]` | readback bits 4 and 5 | crossbar outputs |
| `uo_out[6]` | readback bit 6 | interrupt to the host, or crossbar output |
| `uo_out[7]` | readback bit 7 | trigger out, or crossbar output |
| `uio[0]` | loader strobe (input) | crossbar pad |
| `uio[1]` | loader frame (input) | crossbar pad |
| `uio[7:2]` | unused (inputs) | crossbar pads, push-pull or open drain |

The `PIN_OPT` register sets the run-mode choices: the host SPI interface is on after every load and can be turned off by an image that needs every pin (the host then has no access until the next load); the interrupt pin and the trigger pin are off unless the image turns them on. With the SPI interface on, the lanes have 5 inputs, 7 outputs (5 with interrupt and trigger) and 8 bidirectional pads.

The RP2350 must return GPIO25 and GPIO26 (`uio[0]` and `uio[1]`) to inputs after a load, because the chip may drive them in run mode. The host library does this.

### Loading

The loader commands are: `BEGIN` (stop the lanes, clear the configuration, start a new CRC), `BIST` (run the SRAM self-test), `WR` and `WRB` (write one word or a burst), `RD` and `RDB` (read back), `COMMIT` (compare the host's CRC-32 with the chip's; only a match marks the chip loaded) and `STAT` (status byte). The CRC covers every address and data byte written since `BEGIN`. After `COMMIT` the host reads back the programs and lane configuration and compares them; chip configuration words (crossbar, pin options, event and trigger settings) read as 0 and are protected by the CRC only. A full load takes well under 1 ms of chip time (estimate); the RP2350 drives the byte bus from a PIO state machine.

### Runtime SPI

SPI mode 0, most significant bit first, on the RP2350's hardware SPI0 pins: CS_N `ui_in[0]` (GPIO17), SCK `ui_in[1]` (GPIO18), MOSI `ui_in[2]` (GPIO19), MISO `uo_out[3]` (GPIO36). The frames use the loader's command bytes; a read sends one dummy byte after the address. The pins are sampled by the project clock, so SCK must stay at or below about 1/8 of the clock; the host library uses 4 MHz. While any lane runs, `BEGIN`, `BIST` and `COMMIT` are refused. Through SPI the host starts and stops lanes, exchanges mailbox words with them, reads status and pops events.

### Compared with PIO and PRU

The RP2040 and RP2350 PIO blocks and the TI PRU also run protocols in software, and the programmer counts their cycles. TSE moves that counting into a compiler that refuses a program it cannot prove, adds per-lane hardware for CRC, bit stuffing, line codes, clock recovery and fast responses, and gives each lane fixed memory slots so two protocols cannot disturb each other. PIO is much smaller and a PRU is a full processor; TSE trades both for timing that is stated and checked.

## How to test

Nothing in this section has been run on a board yet; the board code has been checked against the Tiny Tapeout SDK and tested against a stand-in of it. Every step below runs in simulation in the project repository.

Preparation (once):

1. Copy the folders `host/tse_host` and `host/tse_rp2` from the repository to the root of the board's MicroPython file system.
2. Append the section in `host/board/config.ini.part` to the board's `config.ini`. It selects 60 MHz and keeps `rst_n` low after selection.
3. On a PC, build an image: `examples/uart/run.sh` compiles a UART transmitter (lane 0, on `uo_out[0]`) and receiver (lane 1, from `uio[2]`) at 115200 baud and writes `build/e2e/uart/uart.tsei`. Copy that file to the board.

Load and run, from the board's REPL:

```python
from tse_rp2.board import TseBoard
from tse_host import image
b = TseBoard()
name, crc, status, ms = b.load_image("/uart.tsei", run_mask=0b11)
```

`load_image` selects the project, sets the clock from the image, runs the SRAM self-test, loads, checks the CRC, reads back, releases `rst_n` and starts lanes 0 and 1. It raises an error if the self-test fails (use `b.boot_image(image, fallback_image, run_mask=...)` to fall back automatically) or if the CRC or readback does not match.

Send a byte on the UART transmitter:

```python
st = image.chip_addr("MBOX_STATUS")
if not b.session.peek(st)[0] & 0b01:      # lane 0 mailbox free
    b.session.poke(image.chip_addr("MBOX_TX", 0), 0x41)
```

Watch `uo_out[0]` with a logic analyzer or a 3.3 V USB serial adapter (115200 8N1) on the output Pmod: each byte appears as one frame. For the receiver, send 115200 8N1 frames into `uio[2]` from a 3.3 V USB serial adapter on the bidirectional Pmod; when bit 3 of `MBOX_STATUS` is set, the byte waits in `MBOX_RX[1]`, with its bit order mirrored (the lane shifts bits in from the left).

Bring-up order on a new chip: first the SRAM self-test (`load_image` runs it and reports the result register on a failure), then this UART image, then images that use the bidirectional pads.

## External hardware

None for the UART example: the RP2350 is the peer, and a logic analyzer or a 3.3 V USB serial adapter shows the pins. All pins are 3.3 V with no level shifters on the demo board.

Other protocols need a small Pmod each. The parts below are a design note only: none of these circuits has been built, and every part must be checked against its datasheet.

| Protocol | Pmod | Key parts |
|---|---|---|
| SPI | optional | 22 to 47 ohm series resistors on SCK and MOSI, 10 kohm pull-up on CS |
| I2C | yes | pull-ups to 3.3 V: 4.7 kohm (100 kHz) or 2.2 kohm (400 kHz) |
| JTAG and SWD | yes | 10-pin debug header, 33 to 100 ohm series resistors |
| CAN | yes | one 3.3 V transceiver per node, 120 ohm termination at each end |
| USB low speed | yes | switched 1.5 kohm pull-up on D-, 22 to 68 ohm series resistors |
| 10BASE-T | yes | transceiver, magnetics jack, coupling capacitors and termination |
| PS/2 | yes | open-drain level shifter to 5 V per line, pull-ups on both sides |

Avoid Pmod lines that drive `uio[0]` or `uio[1]`: those are loader inputs in load mode and would fight the RP2350.
